Description: Allow the RPi V4L2 decoder and its GBM backend through the sandbox
 Chromium's Linux GPU sandbox is enabled before the sandboxed GPU process
 enumerates decoder profiles. The media layer opens /dev/video10 on a Pi, but
 the ordinary Linux broker does not allow that path, so the open logs
 Permission denied and advertised decoder configs remain empty. Grant
 read/write access to the decoder nodes V4L2Device::GetDevicesForType()
 reports, so the sandbox policy cannot drift from the device the decoder picks.
 Raspberry Pi OS's Mesa libgbm also discovers dri_gbm.so lazily after the
 sandbox starts; preload the backends found alongside libgbm itself, and allow
 them through the broker as well - preloading alone is not enough, because
 glibc still opens the requested path to notice they are already resident.
Author: Tian Zheng <naitgnehz@gmail.com>
Author: Serge Schneider <serge@raspberrypi.com>
Bug: https://github.com/RPi-Distro/chromium/issues/63
Forwarded: https://github.com/RPi-Distro/chromium/pull/64
Last-Update: 2026-08-01

